[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue with one fix queued. The reports do not. They describe
three problems, and the queued parser fix closes only one of them.

## The problems

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **Parser.** The shared parser reads every date month first, so a day-first tenant's dates come
  out flipped: 7 April becomes 4 July. This affects every tenant, through the app, email and the
  booking-v2 form.
- **Form hint.** The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what it
  says, and the form then reads it the tenant's way. The result looks the same as the parser bug,
  but it comes only through the form on booking-v3 tenants. A parser fix does not touch it. The
  hint has to change.
- **One-day moves.** A date moves by a day, or differs between two people for the same meeting.
  Swapping day and month cannot turn 12 May into 11 May, so the parser does not explain these. We
  do not have the cause yet. The Lisbon and Toronto report points at time zones, but that is a
  lead, not a finding.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close the form hint or one-day tickets when the parser fix ships. Check each one on its own.
</Callout>

## The desk's table

The counts above are the desk's, from the table below. `causes.ts` files each report by tenant, form
and channel, so the one-day moves stay in the `parser` row and appear in the note under the table.
Their ticket overlaps the parser ticket: do not add its count on top. Neither table uses `intakeTag`,
which intake guesses from the reporter's browser.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports on subject alone, and nobody
reads the body. Reporters describe the symptom, and all three problems have the same symptom: the
wrong day. So the same subjects appear across them, and subject matching cannot separate them.

## How to check this

- Count by report id. The export repeats some reports across its pages.
- Use the tenant directory for the locale and form, not the browser locale on the report.
- The one-day list is in `desk-notes.json`. Read those reports yourself: a few of them are thin, and
  more reports could move the picture.

